{"body": "These are stipulated finite cases for this task, not observations about any previously described warehouse. Quantities are integer reservation units. Each case names its own information and calculation; additional commitments need to be identified if used.\n\nCase A. A reporting file has capacity 9. Its selected entries are an active hold of 6 for booking K and an active hold of 5 for booking L. Each booking has exactly that one selected entry, and there are no ties, releases or expired holds in this file. The reporting specification says: subtract the sum of the selected quantities from capacity and return the resulting integer. No separate inspection report accompanies case A.\n\nCase B. A snapshot file has capacity 23. Booking U has alternatives 0 or 8, and booking V has alternatives 0 or 11. The reporting procedure forms every pair by choosing one listed quantity for U and one for V; for each pair (u,v) it displays 23-u-v. The file includes no physical-resource fields.\n\nFor case B there is also a distinct inspection file. It stipulates that U=8 and V=11 both need the same single-use jig at the same time, so that pair cannot be performed jointly. Each of the other listed pairs can be performed jointly. The inspection is supplied evidence for this finite case only. A planner asks for the values of 23-u-v for pairs that can be performed jointly.\n\nBefore the inspection file was available, a reviewer of case B wrote: “Each listed quantity may be defensible on its own, yet a pairing might fail to be jointly performable. A resource that the snapshot does not name might matter.” The reviewer's remark itself supplies no observed resource and no identified impossible pair. It is recorded separately from the inspection file.\n", "commitments": "These are stipulated finite cases for this task, not observations about any previously described warehouse. Quantities are integer reservation units. Each case names its own information and calculation; additional comm